<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernst Johann von Biron</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1690">1690</a>–<a href="/years/1772">1772</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ernst Johann von Bühren</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernst Johann von Biron was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> nobleman who became the favourite of Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-russia">Anna of Russia</a> and, with her backing, Duke of Courland and Semigallia in 1737. He commissioned Rastrelli to build Rundāle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Jelgava</a> palaces. Briefly regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> after Anna's death in 1740, he was overthrown within weeks and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, returning to rule Courland from 1763 to 1769.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernst-johann-von-biron.jpg" alt="Ernst Johann von Biron" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernst_Johann_von_Biron_111.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ernst Johann von Bühren was born at Kalnciems in Semigallia in 1690, the second son of Cornet Karl von Bühren. His grandfather had been a groom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-kettler">Jacob Kettler</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, and received a small estate. He studied at the academy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> but was expelled for riotous conduct.</p><p>In 1714 he sought a place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> without success and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Mitau</a>, where a sister who served Anna, regent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, helped him to a place at court. He then supplanted the ruling minister, Peter Bestuzhev, in Anna's favour and had him disgraced and banished. In 1723 he married Benigna Gottlieb von Trotha genannt Treyden, who shared his later exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After Anna became Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1730, Biron was appointed grand chamberlain and made a count of the Empire, receiving an estate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesis">Wenden</a>; his name was now written Biron, and he is said to have adopted the arms of the French ducal house of that name, to which he had no connection. When the Council tried to secure an aristocratic constitution, Anna crushed the Dolgoruki and Galitsin nobles and relied on him.</p><p>He is said to have caused over 1,000 executions and the exile of 20,000 to 40,000 people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and Russians called the period the Bironovshchina. He was nonetheless an able administrator who kept order. In June 1737 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> estates were pressed, with bribes, into electing him duke after the Kettler line died out, and he was invested at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> in 1739.</p><p>On her deathbed Anna named him regent for the infant Ivan VI, and a declaration signed by 194 dignitaries confirmed it on 26 October 1740. Anna died on 28 October. At midnight on 19 November 1740 Field Marshal Münnich seized him in his bedroom. A commission condemned him on 11 April 1741 to death by quartering, commuted by the regent Anna Leopoldovna to banishment at Pelym in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, and his property was confiscated. Empress Elizabeth later moved him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Peter III summoned him to court in 1762, and in 1763 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> restored him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, which he bequeathed to his son Peter in 1769. The last years of his rule were described as just and even benevolent, though autocratic. He died on 29 December 1772 at Rastrelli's palace in Mitava, and his wife outlived him by ten years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ernst Johann von Biron” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 18th century Russian influence grew; <b>Ernst Johann von Biron</b>, favourite of Empress Anna, became duke in 1737 and built Rundāle Palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tīreļi, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Jelgava</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.84,23.59&amp;year=1743" aria-label="Tīreļi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5059 234 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ernst Johann von Biron"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5509 504h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5509 504h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.84,23.59&amp;year=1743">What was here in 1743? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
